# Which architectures each of robovast's own images is built for.
#
# One source of truth, sourced by container/release_images.sh and read by
# .github/workflows/image.yml, so a local build and a CI build cannot disagree about
# what an image is. Encoding it in both places is how they drift.
#
# The rule is where the image *runs*, not what is convenient to build:
#
#   robovast           developers run this locally (including on arm64 Macs) AND it runs as
#                        a job pod in the cluster -> both architectures.
#
#                        This was listed as both for a long time while only one was real. The
#                        base was osrf/ros:<distro>-desktop-full, which upstream publishes for
#                        amd64 alone -- the tag is a single manifest, not an index -- so an
#                        arm64 build took amd64 content and labelled it arm64. It succeeded (on
#                        an amd64 runner nothing is emulated, so even the RUN steps passed) and
#                        shipped an image that dies with "Invalid ELF image for this
#                        architecture" the moment a real arm64 host execs in it. The same class
#                        of silent mislabelling the CLUSTER_PLATFORM note below warns about, in
#                        the other direction.
#
#                        Fixed by moving to ros:<distro>-ros-base, a genuine multi-arch index,
#                        and installing the desktop-full set from the pinned snapshot on top.
#                        A platform listed here is now built on a runner of that architecture
#                        (.github/workflows/image.yml maps them) and the merge step asserts the
#                        published index really carries it -- so this file can no longer promise
#                        an architecture that does not exist.
#   roqsim             both, now. It was narrowed to amd64 solely because of the base described
#                        above, and that reason went with the base. What it adds on top follows:
#                        mujoco and opencv-python-headless publish manylinux aarch64 wheels, and
#                        arm64 is tier-1 for Jazzy on Noble, so the debs are there too. Upstream
#                        roqsim publishes its own images for both architectures on the same
#                        reasoning (cps-test-lab/roqsim#10).
#
#                        Proved rather than assumed, as the note here used to ask for: each
#                        architecture builds on a runner native to it and then execs inside the
#                        image it just built -- `uname -m` against the runner's, and an import of
#                        mujoco -- before anything is merged into a tag.
#   controller, sidecar  only ever exist in the cluster, which is always linux/amd64.
#                        The controller used to have a second reason -- the nav extra pulled
#                        PySide6, which ships no linux-aarch64 wheels. That is gone with the
#                        desktop tools, so nothing now *prevents* an arm64 controller; it is
#                        still built amd64-only because that is where it runs, and widening it
#                        should be a change someone makes with a CI build to prove it.
#
# The architecture every cluster runs, and therefore what `--push` must produce
# regardless of the machine doing the pushing: a `docker build` on an arm64 Mac
# otherwise publishes an image no node can run, and the failure is an obscure
# exec-format error in a pod rather than an error at push time.
CLUSTER_PLATFORM=linux/amd64

PLATFORMS_ROBOVAST=linux/amd64,linux/arm64
PLATFORMS_ROQSIM=linux/amd64,linux/arm64
PLATFORMS_CONTROLLER=linux/amd64
PLATFORMS_SIDECAR=linux/amd64
